"The fast Slepian transform - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1063520317300763)\nciteturn232search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Date: May 2019 ... # The fast Slepian transform☆Author links open overlay panel Santhosh Karnik ^{a}, Zhihui Zhu ^{b}, Michael B. ... We show that there exist fast constructions for computing approximate projections onto the leading Slepian basis elements.\n\n## Applied and Computational Harmonic Analysis\n\nDate: May 2019\n\nPages: 624-652\n\nVolume: Volume 46, Issue 3\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\nResearch article\n\nOpen archive\n\nUnder an Elsevier user license\n\nGet rights and content\n\n# The fast Slepian transform☆\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Santhosh Karnik ^{a}, Zhihui Zhu ^{b}, Michael B. Wakin ^{b}, Justin Romberg ^{a}, Mark A. Davenport ^{a}\n\nShow more\n\nView PDF Download full issueView Open Manuscript\n\n\n10.1016/j.acha.2017.07.005\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe discrete prolate spheroidal sequences (DPSS's) provide an efficient representation for discrete signals that are perfectly timelimited and nearly bandlimited. Due to the high computational complexity of projecting onto the DPSS basis – also known as the Slepian basis – this representation is often overlooked in favor of the fast Fourier transform (FFT). We show that there exist fast constructions for computing approximate projections onto the leading Slepian basis elements. The complexity of the resulting algorithms is comparable to the FFT, and scales favorably as the quality of the desired approximation is increased. In the process of bounding the complexity of these algorithms, we also establish new nonasymptotic results on the eigenvalue distribution of discrete time–frequency localization operators. We then demonstrate how these algorithms allow us to efficiently compute the solution to certain least-squares problems that arise in signal processing. We also provide simulations comparing these fast, approximate Slepian methods to exact Slepian methods as well as the traditional FFT based methods.\n\n## Keywords\n\nSlepian basis\n\n;\n\nDiscrete prolate spheroidal sequences\n\n;\n\nTime–frequency analysis\n\n;\n\nFast Fourier transform\n\n;\n\nSignal approximation and compression\n\n^{☆}\n\nThis work was supported by NSF grants CCF-1409261 and CCF-1409406. A preliminary version of this paper highlighting some of the key results also appeared in [1].--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSanthosh Karnik - Publications (https://sites.google.com/view/santhoshkarnik/publications)\nciteturn232search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Davenport, \"The Fast Slepian Transform.\" ... Conf. on Acoustics, Speech, and Signal Processing (ICASSP), Singapore, May 2022. ... Sampling Theory and its Applications (SampTA), Bordeaux, France, July 2019. ... Karnik, \"Fast Algorithms for Sampled Multiband Signals,\" Ph.D.\nApplied and Computational Harmonic Analysis, 55(1), pp. 97-128, November 2021. [arXiv]\n\n  * S. Karnik, Z. Zhu, M. B. Wakin, J. Romberg, and M. A. Davenport, \"The Fast Slepian Transform.\" Applied and Computational Harmonic Analysis, 46(3), pp. 624-652, May 2019. [arXiv]\n\n  * Z. Zhu, S. Karnik, M. B. Wakin, M. A. Davenport, and J. Romberg, \"ROAST: Rapid Orthogonal Approximate Slepian Transform.\" IEEE Trans. on Signal Processing, 66(22), pp. 5887-5901, November 2018. [arXiv]\n\n  * Z. Zhu, S. Karnik, M. A. Davenport, J. Romberg, and M. B. Wakin, \"The Eigenvalue Distribution of Discrete Periodic Time-Frequency Limiting Operators.\" IEEE Signal Processing Letters, 25(1), pp. 95-99, January 2018. [arXiv]\n\nConference Papers\n\n  * S. Karnik, A. Veselovska, M. Iwen, F. Krahmer, \"Implicit Regularization for Tubal Tensor Factorizations via Gradient Descent.\" in Proc. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Fast Slepian Transform (https://arxiv.org/abs/161